<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Acajutla</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Acajutla is a port town on the Pacific coast of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> and the country's main seaport. In June 1524 the Spanish conquistador <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a> fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pipil">Pipil</a> there in the first major battle of the Spanish conquest of El Salvador; Alvarado won but was badly wounded in the leg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acajutla.jpg" alt="Acajutla" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: U.S. Navy photo by Mass Communication Specialist 2nd Class Joshua Karsten · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:US_Navy_070730-N-8704K-053_Hospital_ship_USNS_Comfort_(T-AH_20)_is_moored_in_Acajutla,_El_Salvador,_during_a_scheduled_port_visit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Acajutla is a seaport city in Sonsonate Department, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>, on the Pacific coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>. It is one of seventeen districts of Sonsonate and the country's principal seaport. A large part of the nation's exports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and Balsam of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> is shipped from it. As on the whole Pacific coast of Central America, the climate is continuously hot and humid, with daytime highs usually between 32 and 34 °C; it is classed as a tropical savanna climate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a>, a Spanish conquistador under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a>, had conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> before he came to the vicinity of Acajutla. There he met heavy resistance but defeated the indigenous people in 1524 and conquered all of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> at the Battle of Acajutla. After El Salvador's complete independence in 1838 its economy became increasingly dependent on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> exports. The growth of this cash crop led to deep socio-economic changes and drew foreign investors and local plantation owners to Acajutla, where infrastructure was needed to carry the crop from the interior and load it onto ships.</p><p>During the peasant uprising of 1932 two destroyers of the Royal Canadian Navy, HMCS Skeena and HMCS Vancouver, anchored off Acajutla at the request of the British Consul, who feared for British nationals and assets. Armed Canadian sailors briefly landed against the wishes of the Salvadoran government and prepared to go on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, but the situation improved and the British no longer thought an armed presence necessary. During the civil war of 1980-1992 the oil refinery at Acajutla, then the only one operating in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>, was a target for anti-government rebels.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The port was built in its first phase at a cost of 25 million colones. Its breakwater of 338 metres began operations in August 1961, and it is managed by the Executive Commission of Autonomous Port (CEPA). The deepwater harbour handles a large part of the country's major exports, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> making up 40 percent of its cargo, and the oil refinery, the nation's largest, mainly refines petroleum imported from Venezuela. The town holds its traditional festivals from the last days of May to 2 June, and on 24 October fishermen celebrate in honour of San Rafael Arcangel. Between March and April people decorate the streets with sand brought from the beach and enact the passion of Christ.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Acajutla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1524 Pedro de Alvarado invaded and fought the Pipil at <b>Acajutla</b> and Tacuzcalco, but they resisted for years; San Salvador, first founded in 1525, was re-established in 1528.</blockquote><a href="/state/cuzcatlan">Cuzcatlan</a></li></ul></section>
